(Now we need to ensure we have not set vehicle_allocated for origin/destination after dispatch? The vehicle_allocated predicate remains true after dispatch? The execute_*_dispatch effect does not mention vehicle_allocated. So vehicle_allocated remains true. That's okay; we may have vehicle_allocated true and vehicle_available true simultaneously? That would be inconsistent but maybe allowed? In PDDL, having both true could be contradictory but not prevented. However typical domain expects vehicle_allocated implies not vehicle_available. But the action does not retract vehicle_allocated. This could lead to state where both are true. But we may still be able to allocate another vehicle to same candidate? allocate_vehicle_unit requires not vehicle_allocated ?candidate. So if vehicle_allocated stays true, we cannot reallocate same vehicle to same candidate, but we can allocate a different vehicle unit to same candidate? The predicate vehicle_allocated ?candidate ?vehicle_unit? Actually vehicle_allocated is unary? Wait predicate definition: (vehicle_allocated ?candidate - candidate_entity). It does not include vehicle unit. So vehicle_allocated just indicates that some vehicle is allocated to candidate, not which one. So after dispatch, vehicle_allocated remains true, meaning we think a vehicle is still allocated, but vehicle_available becomes true again (contradiction). This may break allocate_vehicle_unit precondition (requires not vehicle_allocated ?candidate). So after dispatch, we cannot allocate another vehicle to same candidate because vehicle_allocated still true. But we don't need to allocate again to same candidate. For other candidates, vehicle_allocated is false initially, so we can allocate.)
(Thus after we dispatch origin_availability_alpha, vehicle_allocated origin_availability_alpha stays true, but we won't need to allocate another vehicle to it. Good.)
(Thus we can reuse the same vehicle unit for other candidates because vehicle_allocated is per candidate, not per vehicle unit. The vehicle unit availability is tracked via vehicle_available predicate. So after dispatch we set vehicle_available true again, and vehicle_allocated remains true for that candidate (which is okay). So we can allocate a different vehicle unit to another candidate.)
(Thus we can allocate vehicle_unit_alpha to origin, after dispatch origin we get vehicle_available α true, but vehicle_allocated origin still true. Then we can allocate vehicle_unit_alpha to another candidate? allocate_vehicle_unit requires vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit and not vehicle_allocated ?candidate. It does not check that the vehicle unit is not already allocated to some other candidate. So we could allocate same vehicle unit to multiple candidates simultaneously, which would be unrealistic but allowed by the domain. However we should avoid that to keep plan sensible, but not required.)
(Thus we can allocate vehicle_unit_alpha to origin, then after dispatch we still have vehicle_allocated origin true, but we can allocate vehicle_unit_alpha to destination as well (since vehicle_available α true and vehicle_allocated destination false). That would allocate same vehicle unit to two candidates concurrently. Might be okay but could cause weirdness. Safer to use different vehicles.)
(Thus we'll use distinct vehicles for each candidate: origin uses α, destination uses β, shift_alpha uses γ, shift_beta uses α after origin dispatch frees it (but vehicle_allocated origin still true, but we can still allocate α to beta; vehicle_allocated beta false, vehicle_available α true). This would mean vehicle α allocated to both origin and beta simultaneously (vehicle_allocated origin true, vehicle_allocated beta true). That's okay per domain but maybe not intended. However we can also use vehicle β for beta after destination dispatch frees it. Let's do that: after dispatch destination, vehicle β becomes available, allocate to beta. That way each vehicle used by at most one candidate at a time.)
(Thus we need to dispatch origin and destination before preparing beta.)
(Thus order:)
(- Prepare origin, destination, shift_alpha.)
(- Activate origin node, assign operator, lock origin.)
(- Activate destination node, assign operator, lock destination.)
(- Assemble transport leg.)
(- Authorize loading.)
(- Load alpha container onto leg.)
(- Stage alpha.)
(- Verify loading profile nominal (alpha).)
(- Assign compliance certificate to alpha.)
(- Establish clearance nominal (alpha).)
(- Release skill certificate (alpha).)
(- Release compliance certificate (alpha).)
(- Finalize candidate direct (alpha).)
(- Allocate dispatch slot for origin.)
(- Execute origin dispatch.)
(- Allocate dispatch slot for destination.)
(- Execute destination dispatch.)
(- Allocate dispatch slot for shift_alpha.)
(- Execute schedule dispatch for shift_alpha.)
(- (After origin and destination dispatched, vehicles α and β become available.))
(- Prepare beta: allocate vehicle (say α), attach task profile, confirm prepared.)
(- Assign operator to beta.)
(- Assign skill certificate to beta.)
(- Load beta container onto leg.)
(- Stage beta.)
(- (Goal requires candidate_staged_for_loading beta, operator linked, skill cert linked.))
(- Done.)
